findings.description:
Lines 133-136 say a PR review "satisfies the review frontmatter contract" and that archiving, digest and integrity checks "apply unchanged". That contract isn't written down anywhere, and today's writer is slice-shaped throughout:
- frontmatter `slice:` and `project:` (`persistence.py:206-216`);
- the heading `# Review: {type} — slice {index}` (line 266);
- the filename `{index}-review.{type}.{slice}` (line 497).

Several readers depend on these names:
- `sq review resolve` and `resolution_artifact.py:26-29` (`{index}-resolution.{type}.{slice_name}-r*`);
- metrology discovery globbing `*-review.*` (`metrology/discovery.py:46`);
- findings-addressed evidence;
- `cf` artifact scanning;
- slice 917's commit verdict gate.

The document needs to say what replaces `slice:` / `project:`, whether a PR name should match the `*-review.*` glob, and whether `sq review resolve` works on PR reviews. This is the main decision in the persistence slice, and it is currently left out.

The document says a PR review "passes through the same parser, rules loading, scope assertion, and tool wiring". Except for the parser, that logic lives inline in the `review_code` Typer command body (`review.py:976-1094`):
- diff normalization;
- `assert_reviewable_scope`;
- the rules choice (flag > config) and language auto-detection via `extract_diff_paths`;
- building `inputs`;
- the save outcome.

None of it is a callable function. `sq review pr` either copies it, which creates the second reviewer the document rules out, or needs `review_code` split into a reusable function first. That refactor changes `sq review code`, which line 42 promises won't change. Neither slice mentions it. A pure "resolve, then call the existing engine" design also shouldn't add more PR logic to a 1,200-line `review.py`. The orchestration belongs in its own module.
